WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.761
An agent can't be asked
for a second factor,

2
00:00:02.761 --> 00:00:05.950
so everything it may do is
decided before it asks.

3
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.150
Credentials first: a managed identity or
federation, so there's no stored secret;

4
00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:16.350
a certificate only where neither works;
and a client secret never in production.

5
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.600
Then permissions.

6
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.562
Microsoft blocks the very
top roles for agents,

7
00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:24.512
but still lets one hold Exchange
or SharePoint administrator.

8
00:00:24.512 --> 00:00:26.837
That's a floor, not least privilege.

9
00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:31.348
So the book's rule is no standing
privileged role for any AI identity,

10
00:00:31.348 --> 00:00:34.893
and a policy that blocks
every agent not yet approved,

11
00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:37.712
kept in report-only mode
while it's preview.

12
00:00:37.712 --> 00:00:39.738
The CISO sets those requirements,

13
00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:44.060
and identity engineering builds them,
before the first production agent.
